{
  "question": "In the context of formal verification for high-complexity automated systems modeled using Petri Nets, a supervisor must choose between using General Petri Nets and Bounded Petri Nets. Given the inherent theorem regarding computational limits, which structural constraint allows the Reachability Problem to become decidable, thereby enabling exhaustive verification of Deadlock-Free (Liveness) and Resource-Bounded (Safety) properties?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "A. The constraint that every transition must fire in a single instant (Synchronization)",
      "id": "A"
    },
    {
      "text": "B. The impositon of a maximum finite token limit on every place in the net structure (Boundedness)",
      "id": "B"
    },
    {
      "text": "C. The restriction that every place holds exactly one token (Safe net)",
      "id": "C"
    },
    {
      "text": "D. The requirement that the system state space must be infinite to cover all future scenarios",
      "id": "D"
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": "B",
  "explanation": "While the Reachability Problem for General Petri Nets is computationally undecidable (due to potentially infinite firing sequences), it becomes decidable and the state space becomes finite when the Petri Net is Bounded. This structural constraint ensures that the number of possible markings is finite, allowing for the application of exhaustive reachability algorithms to verify system properties such as liveness and safety. Option C is a subset of boundedness (safe means bounded by 1), but general boundedness is the theoretical prerequisite for decidability. Option A describes a timing constraint not inherent to the basic decidable state space argument. Option D contradicts the necessity of finite states for decidability.",
  "topic": "Petri Nets in Automation",
  "difficulty": "PhD",
  "subcategory": "Discrete Event Systems / Formal Verification"
}

